    "source": "Government of India Budget at a Glance (indiabudget.gov.in). Aggregate figures for 2024-25 RE and 2025-26 RE verified against the Budget at a Glance 2026-27 document. 2026-27 aggregates from the same source. 2025-26 and 2024-25 are Revised Estimates; earlier years are Actuals. Historical detail heads are provisional rounded estimates. Transfers to states captures grants-in-aid only; excludes tax devolution (~1.4 L cr in 2025-26 RE). Verify all figures against official GoI documents before publication.",
    "cpi_note": "CPI index values (base 2011-12 = 100) used for inflation adjustment. Approximate series; use RBI or MOSPI official data for precision.",
    "cpi_base_fy": "2025-26",
    "cpi_base_value": 220.0
  },
  "heads": [
    {
      "id": "total_expenditure",
      "label": "Total Expenditure",
      "group": "expenditure",
      "short": "Total Exp.",
      "desc": "The full planned spending by the central government in the financial year, across all ministries and departments. This is the headline number in the Budget."
    },
    {
      "id": "revenue_expenditure",
      "label": "Revenue Expenditure",
      "group": "expenditure",
      "short": "Revenue Exp.",
      "desc": "Day-to-day spending that does not create assets — salaries, pensions, interest payments, subsidies and transfers. Most government spending falls here."
    },
    {
      "id": "capital_expenditure",
      "label": "Capital Expenditure",
      "group": "expenditure",
      "short": "Capital Exp.",
      "desc": "Spending that creates physical or financial assets — roads, railways, hospitals, equity in public enterprises. Higher capex generally signals longer-term investment."
    },
    {
      "id": "interest_payments",
      "label": "Interest Payments",
      "group": "expenditure",
      "short": "Interest",
      "desc": "Interest paid on the government's accumulated debt. This is a mandatory charge — it must be paid before anything else — and grows every year borrowings rise."
    },
    {
      "id": "defence",
      "label": "Defence",
      "group": "expenditure",
      "short": "Defence",
      "desc": "Allocation for the Army, Navy, Air Force, defence research (DRDO) and defence capital — procurement of equipment and infrastructure."
    },
    {
      "id": "agriculture",
      "label": "Agriculture & Rural",
      "group": "expenditure",
      "short": "Agriculture",
      "desc": "Covers farm support (PM-KISAN, crop insurance), irrigation, and rural livelihood programmes (MGNREGS). A large share of India's workforce depends on this allocation."
    },
    {
      "id": "education",
      "label": "Education",
      "group": "expenditure",
      "short": "Education",
      "desc": "Central allocation for school education (Samagra Shiksha), higher education (IITs, IIMs, UGC) and skill development. State governments add substantially on top of this."
    },
    {
      "id": "health",
      "label": "Health",
      "group": "expenditure",
      "short": "Health",
      "desc": "Covers the National Health Mission, AIIMS-type hospitals, health research (ICMR) and schemes like Ayushman Bharat. As with education, states spend considerably more."
    },
    {
      "id": "subsidies",
      "label": "Subsidies",
      "group": "expenditure",
      "short": "Subsidies",
      "desc": "Mainly food (FCI and PDS), fertiliser and petroleum subsidies. These can swing sharply with global commodity prices — the 2008 and 2021 spikes were largely subsidy-driven."
    },
    {
      "id": "transfers_to_states",
      "label": "Transfers to States",
      "group": "expenditure",
      "short": "State Transfers",
      "desc": "Grants-in-aid paid by the central government to states, covering tied scheme funding and other non-tax grants. This does not include tax devolution (states' share of central taxes under the Finance Commission formula), which is the larger component of total centre-to-state transfers."
    },
    {
      "id": "receipts_excl_borrowing",
      "label": "Receipts excl. Borrowings",
      "group": "receipts",
      "short": "Receipts",
      "desc": "All government income before borrowings — tax revenue (income tax, GST, corporate tax) plus non-tax revenue (dividends, fees, spectrum auctions). The 'own resources' of the government.",
      "chart1": false
    },
    {
      "id": "fiscal_deficit",
      "label": "Fiscal Deficit",
      "group": "fiscal",
      "short": "Fiscal Deficit",
      "desc": "The gap between total expenditure and receipts excluding borrowings. It equals the amount the government must borrow that year. Expressed as % of GDP it is the main measure of fiscal health."
    },
    {
      "id": "income_tax",
      "label": "Income Tax",
      "group": "receipts_detail",
      "short": "Inc. Tax",
      "desc": "Personal income tax collected from individuals, HUFs, and firms. Growing share of India's direct taxes as more people enter the tax net and incomes rise."
    },
    {
      "id": "corporation_tax",
      "label": "Corporation Tax",
      "group": "receipts_detail",
      "short": "Corp. Tax",
      "desc": "Tax on domestic and foreign company profits. A major revenue pillar — its share in the tax mix fell after the 2019 rate cut from 30% to 22% for existing companies."
    },
    {
      "id": "indirect_taxes",
      "label": "Indirect Taxes",
      "group": "receipts_detail",
      "short": "Indirect",
      "desc": "Customs duties, central GST (post-2017), and central excise. Before GST, this included service tax and additional excise duties. The 2016–17 spike reflects the last full pre-GST year."
    },
    {
      "id": "non_tax_revenue",
      "label": "Non-Tax Revenue",
      "group": "receipts_detail",
      "short": "Non-Tax",
      "desc": "Dividends from PSUs and the RBI, interest receipts on government loans, fees, fines, spectrum auction proceeds and other miscellaneous receipts — collected without a tax."
    }
  ],
